==============================================================
=/                      V P O R T                           \=
==============================================================

      A tiny, deterministic display protocol and server
         for embedded and point-of-sale Linux systems


--[ 1. WHAT IS VPORT ]-----------------------------------------

VPort is a minimal graphics protocol and display server for
Linux. A client (a script, a point-of-sale program, a kiosk
application) talks to the server through a Unix domain socket
using a small binary wire protocol, and the server draws what
the client asks for in an isolated "portal" (viewport).

VPort is NOT a replacement for X11 or Wayland on the desktop.
It does not run browsers, IDEs or toolkits. It exists for
machines that show a handful of well defined screens: cash
registers, self-service totems, information panels, embedded
controllers and terminal scripts.


--[ 2. DESIGN GOALS ]------------------------------------------

  Small      The whole graphical stack should fit in less than
             20 MB of RAM. A typical X11 + Chromium kiosk setup
             needs between 400 MB and 1 GB per terminal.

  Isolated   Every client owns its portals and can see nothing
             else. There is no screen scraping and no global
             key grabbing. Clients are identified by the
             kernel (SO_PEERCRED), not by what they claim.

  Resilient  Application logic (client) and rendering (server)
             are strictly separated. The server must be able
             to restart in milliseconds without taking the
             application down.

  Simple     Fixed size binary commands, one thread, one
             epoll loop, a handful of dependencies, hard
             limits on everything a client can allocate.


--[ 3. NON-GOALS ]---------------------------------------------

  - Running generic desktop applications.
  - Window management, decorations, compositing effects.
  - Network transparency.
  - Rendering HTML, CSS or any markup.


--[ 4. COMPONENTS ]--------------------------------------------

  vportd       The server daemon. Listens on a Unix socket,
               validates every message, keeps the state of
               each portal and presents committed frames.

  libvport     The client library. Connects to vportd and
               exposes calls such as init_portal, draw_rect
               and commit. Written in Rust, a C ABI is
               planned for C, Python and shell bindings.

  vport-proto  The wire protocol shared by both sides.
               See doc/PROTOCOL.


--[ 5. SOURCE TREE ]-------------------------------------------

  vport/
  +-- README
  +-- CREDITS
  +-- CONTRIBUTING
  +-- Cargo.toml
  +-- doc/
  |   +-- PROTOCOL        wire protocol specification
  +-- lib/
  |   +-- vport-proto/    shared protocol definitions
  |   +-- libvport/       client library and examples
  +-- programs/
      +-- vportd/         the display server daemon


--[ 6. BUILDING ]----------------------------------------------

  A recent stable Rust toolchain is the only requirement.

      $ cargo build --release
      $ cargo test


--[ 7. RUNNING ]-----------------------------------------------

  The default socket is /run/vport/vport.sock, which needs
  root. For development use a socket in /tmp:

      $ export VPORT_SOCKET=/tmp/vport-dev.sock
      $ cargo run -p vportd

  and in another terminal:

      $ export VPORT_SOCKET=/tmp/vport-dev.sock
      $ cargo run -p libvport --example demo

  Optional environment for vportd:

      VPORT_BACKEND    "ppm" or "sdl2" (default: sdl2 when built
                       with the sdl2 feature, otherwise ppm)
      VPORT_PPM        file written by the ppm backend
                       (default /tmp/vport-frame.ppm)
      VPORT_SIZE       screen size as WIDTHxHEIGHT
                       (default 800x480)
      VPORT_GRAB_UIDS  comma separated uids allowed to grab the
                       keyboard (default: 0 and the uid of
                       vportd)

  To get a window with the SDL2 backend (needs the SDL2
  development package):

      $ cargo run -p vportd --features sdl2

  Closing the window or pressing Escape stops vportd.


--[ 8. STATUS ]------------------------------------------------

  VPort is in early development. The wire protocol (v0), the
  server loop and the client library exist. There is no
  rendering backend yet: committed frames are only kept in
  memory. Planned next:

      - in-memory backend with PPM dump (for testing)
      - SDL2 backend (prototype)
      - DRM/KMS backend
      - text and image commands
      - PointerMove events, evdev input for the DRM
      backend, secure attention key
      - C ABI for libvport


--[ 9. DOCUMENTATION ]-----------------------------------------

